# reviewer-onboard: admit cchifor/${REPO} to the two review bots. Both reviewer identities get write, and `main` gets
# the bots-only rule that cchifor/workbricks-apps and cchifor/relay carry (relay's differs only in its status context).
#   scripts/gitea-owner-op.sh scripts/owner-ops/reviewer-onboard.ops --var REPO=<repository>             # dry run
#   scripts/gitea-owner-op.sh scripts/owner-ops/reviewer-onboard.ops --var REPO=<repository> --execute
# Collaborators and branch protection need repository admin, which the routine identities do not hold: an owner
# operation. The rule requires the context `CI / verify (pull_request)`: the repository needs a workflow `CI` with a
# job `verify`, or nothing can merge (reviewbot also merges only on a combined status of success). The context is
# fixed in the body because a --var value allows only [A-Za-z0-9._-]: a repository with another workflow or job name
# needs its own body. Run this BEFORE the allowlist entry in ansible/roles/pr_reviewer/defaults/main.yml merges: any
# reviewer-host converge after that merge activates the entry, and a private repository is invisible to the reviewers
# until this grants them access. That entry and a reviewer-host converge complete the admission.
# The POST answers 403 on a repository that already has a `main` rule: read it first and edit it deliberately.
# scopes: write:repository
PUT /repos/cchifor/${REPO}/collaborators/reviewer-claude reviewer-onboard-collaborator.json 204
PUT /repos/cchifor/${REPO}/collaborators/reviewer-codex reviewer-onboard-collaborator.json 204
POST /repos/cchifor/${REPO}/branch_protections reviewer-onboard-protection.json 201
